<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naples</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Napoli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naples is the largest city of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>. Founded by Greek settlers from Cumae as Neapolis, it kept its Greek character under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a self-governing Byzantine duchy. From the 13th century it was the capital of the Kingdom of Naples under Angevin, Aragonese, Spanish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> kings, and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s largest cities, until it joined united Italy in 1861.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naples.jpg" alt="Naples" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Nevell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napoli_-_Maschio_Angioino_-_202209302342_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
